   "Long-term rate pricing is primarily driven by Fed policy shifts that challenge market assumptions about the future funds rate distribution, rather than deeper structural macro drivers.",
   "The recent sell-off in bond yields to cycle peaks in October was driven by the Fed's shift to a new hiking cycle, not by fiscal deficits or AI investment demand.",
   "Historical data suggests forward rates anchor near the peak of the most recent Fed hiking cycle; pushing yields meaningfully above 5.3% is unlikely without expectations of further Fed hikes.",
   "The market's assumption that neutral rates are in the 3-3.5% range has been challenged by the policy rate failing to reach those levels before rising again.",
   "Relief in forward rates is likely to occur only when the market gains confidence that the Fed hiking cycle has definitively peaked.",
   "The conflict between 'fiscal deficit/AI investment' and 'Fed policy' as drivers of higher real rates is overstated; policy shifts are a reflection of the economy embedding those structural forces."
